1
00:00:08,369 --> 00:00:12,710
[SPEAKER_03]: I went across to the cigar store where the players hung out.

2
00:00:15,731 --> 00:00:17,311
[SPEAKER_03]: Nobody knew me.

3
00:00:18,011 --> 00:00:18,551
[SPEAKER_03]: At all.

4
00:00:20,252 --> 00:00:25,733
[SPEAKER_03]: And all these players' wives, they really give me a rough time.

5
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:33,034
[SPEAKER_03]: But when the season was over, just the day before the season was over, I called them kids all in.

6
00:00:34,635 --> 00:00:35,515
[SPEAKER_03]: And I said, now look,

7
00:00:37,112 --> 00:00:40,174
[SPEAKER_03]: I told them the reason that day, what I'd done.

8
00:00:41,595 --> 00:00:43,276
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, I did it for your own benefit.

9
00:00:43,796 --> 00:00:46,197
[SPEAKER_03]: As you guys got in the playoffs, you made a little extra money.

10
00:00:47,478 --> 00:00:50,860
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, if I'd have let you guys went along, you'd all been mad at each other.

11
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:53,421
[SPEAKER_03]: One of you would have come up with all the money.

12
00:00:53,501 --> 00:00:54,882
[SPEAKER_03]: The other guys wouldn't have had any.

13
00:00:55,423 --> 00:01:00,105
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, all your wives would have been on me for letting you gamble and all that sort of stuff.

14
00:01:02,327 --> 00:01:04,368
[SPEAKER_03]: I went to the owner and he owned the,

15
00:01:06,152 --> 00:01:12,598
[SPEAKER_03]: His name was Hawkins, I believe, and he owned the radio station.

16
00:01:12,618 --> 00:01:14,519
[SPEAKER_03]: He wanted me to come back the next year, and I knew.

17
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:19,984
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, I wouldn't come back to this town and manage this ball club if you give me your radio station.

18
00:01:20,004 --> 00:01:22,546
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, I've had it.

19
00:01:25,068 --> 00:01:27,150
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, Leo Miller was a good friend of mine.

20
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:30,733
[SPEAKER_03]: He wanted to coach in Annapolis, so I signed with him.

21
00:01:31,675 --> 00:01:33,797
[SPEAKER_03]: went to Indianapolis in 1940.

22
00:01:34,577 --> 00:01:36,499
[SPEAKER_03]: But then I got tired of that and quit.

23
00:01:36,519 --> 00:01:37,879
[SPEAKER_03]: I just came on back home.

24
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:48,107
[SPEAKER_03]: They wanted me to come over and take a job over here at Acme Lease where they make moldings for automobiles and all that stuff.

25
00:01:49,928 --> 00:01:53,470
[SPEAKER_03]: Miss Gracie, the general manager of the plant,

26
00:01:54,962 --> 00:01:59,403
[SPEAKER_03]: Said if I'd come over and start a ball club for him, he'd make me a supervisor.

27
00:02:01,584 --> 00:02:06,885
[SPEAKER_03]: Within less than a year, I was night superintendent of that plant, and I stayed with him 20 years.

28
00:02:06,905 --> 00:02:08,205
[SPEAKER_02]: What was the name of the plant again?

29
00:02:08,365 --> 00:02:09,085
[SPEAKER_02]: Acme Lease.

30
00:02:09,525 --> 00:02:10,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Acme Lease.

31
00:02:11,526 --> 00:02:13,726
[SPEAKER_03]: And where is that in, uh... That was here in Muncie.

32
00:02:13,746 --> 00:02:18,427
[SPEAKER_03]: They're, they're now, they've moved out of Muncie and they've gone to Cullom, Alabama.

33
00:02:20,368 --> 00:02:21,068
[SPEAKER_02]: Labor problems.

34
00:02:22,535 --> 00:02:24,436
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we had labor trouble there.

35
00:02:24,456 --> 00:02:26,918
[SPEAKER_02]: So you stayed there?

36
00:02:26,958 --> 00:02:28,659
[SPEAKER_02]: 20 years, stayed with them.

37
00:02:29,119 --> 00:02:30,400
[SPEAKER_02]: That'd be from 1941?

38
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,121
[SPEAKER_03]: 1940 till I retired.

39
00:02:33,781 --> 00:02:34,482
[SPEAKER_02]: 1960?

40
00:02:35,002 --> 00:02:35,662
[SPEAKER_02]: 60.

41
00:02:41,305 --> 00:02:43,086
[SPEAKER_03]: When did Acme Lease move out, Mom?

42
00:02:43,967 --> 00:02:46,488
[SPEAKER_03]: When did Acme Lease, when did I retire?

43
00:02:46,628 --> 00:02:47,969
[SPEAKER_01]: In 61.

44
00:02:48,129 --> 00:02:48,529
[SPEAKER_03]: 61, yeah.

45
00:02:51,892 --> 00:02:53,593
[SPEAKER_03]: It was 41, I think.

46
00:02:53,713 --> 00:02:57,455
[SPEAKER_03]: Anyway, it was just 20 years.

47
00:02:57,535 --> 00:02:59,616
[SPEAKER_01]: You quit baseball before Robin was born.

48
00:03:00,076 --> 00:03:00,677
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I know.

49
00:03:00,837 --> 00:03:02,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Robin born in 40.

50
00:03:02,598 --> 00:03:03,538
[SPEAKER_03]: I quit in 40.

51
00:03:04,599 --> 00:03:06,560
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the fellow that we met here.

52
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:07,941
[SPEAKER_03]: He was born in 40.

53
00:03:08,321 --> 00:03:10,742
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, he's the only one that never saw me play.

54
00:03:10,762 --> 00:03:16,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he mentioned that.

55
00:03:17,325 --> 00:03:20,527
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, have you kept up your interest in baseball after your...

56
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:22,060
[SPEAKER_02]: retirement from the game?

57
00:03:22,521 --> 00:03:30,723
[SPEAKER_03]: A little bit, not like... Nobody I know anymore.

58
00:03:30,863 --> 00:03:43,048
[SPEAKER_03]: I used to go to Cincinnati and Chicago and used to go to Annapolis quite a bit when I knew a lot of the fellas, but now all the fellas are all dead or too old.

59
00:03:43,068 --> 00:03:45,088
[SPEAKER_03]: They don't... All young guys.

60
00:03:45,108 --> 00:03:45,889
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know nobody.

61
00:03:48,571 --> 00:03:51,532
[SPEAKER_03]: For me to go to St. Magnus and watch a ballgame, I can't do it.

62
00:03:52,412 --> 00:03:52,912
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that right?

63
00:03:53,032 --> 00:03:53,292
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

64
00:03:54,432 --> 00:03:57,853
[SPEAKER_03]: When I go to Cincinnati, I'd go down in the dugout and sit down with the players.

65
00:03:58,073 --> 00:03:58,293
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh.

66
00:03:58,893 --> 00:04:02,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Talk to them, but I couldn't stand that.

67
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:06,815
[SPEAKER_02]: You mean it's uncomfortable, or you just want to be with the players?

68
00:04:06,835 --> 00:04:11,556
[SPEAKER_03]: I just don't get no kick out of it for some reason or another.

69
00:04:11,776 --> 00:04:12,916
[SPEAKER_02]: You're watching on the TV?

70
00:04:13,076 --> 00:04:15,016
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I watch World Series on TV.

71
00:04:15,036 --> 00:04:17,097
[SPEAKER_03]: Once in a while, I'll watch a

72
00:04:18,210 --> 00:04:19,951
[SPEAKER_03]: Monday night game or Saturday game.

73
00:04:21,112 --> 00:04:22,393
[SPEAKER_03]: But outside of that, I don't know.

74
00:04:22,413 --> 00:04:24,314
[SPEAKER_03]: I played too much tennis.

75
00:04:24,334 --> 00:04:26,576
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you read the sporting news much?

76
00:04:26,616 --> 00:04:28,297
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't even take the sporting news anymore.

77
00:04:28,577 --> 00:04:29,258
[SPEAKER_03]: You did take it?

78
00:04:29,918 --> 00:04:31,379
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh yeah, I took it for years.

79
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:34,221
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I just quit.

80
00:04:37,043 --> 00:04:39,105
[SPEAKER_02]: The game's changed a good bit since your day.

81
00:04:39,245 --> 00:04:40,445
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh yeah, yeah.

82
00:04:40,465 --> 00:04:42,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you like some of these changes?

83
00:04:42,967 --> 00:04:43,247
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

84
00:04:43,968 --> 00:04:46,009
[SPEAKER_03]: There's a lot of changes they've made I don't like.

85
00:04:46,069 --> 00:04:46,970
[SPEAKER_03]: What are some of them?

86
00:04:47,242 --> 00:04:50,903
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, one of them is this designated hitter.

87
00:04:51,703 --> 00:04:53,303
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the craziest thing I ever heard of.

88
00:04:56,704 --> 00:04:59,985
[SPEAKER_02]: It's going to lengthen the careers of some players.

89
00:05:01,485 --> 00:05:05,326
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose they'd had that in Ty Cobb or Speaker's Day or Eddie Collins.

90
00:05:06,406 --> 00:05:12,607
[SPEAKER_02]: They'd have gone on and played another ten years and made some more records.

91
00:05:14,928 --> 00:05:15,588
[SPEAKER_03]: Or Ruthie.

92
00:05:16,685 --> 00:05:21,169
[SPEAKER_03]: It bothered, to me, to me, you work around.

93
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:26,172
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't give a manager the opportunity to do the things he wants to do.

94
00:05:27,233 --> 00:05:32,638
[SPEAKER_03]: You can work around and get a pitcher up there to hit, but if you've got to designate a hitter, you can't do that.

95
00:05:32,658 --> 00:05:36,160
[SPEAKER_03]: That pitcher stays there and rests and this other guy comes up and hits for him.

96
00:05:36,961 --> 00:05:41,805
[SPEAKER_03]: It takes a lot of the game away, the scientific part of the game.

97
00:05:42,385 --> 00:05:45,728
[SPEAKER_03]: A lot of times you can work around and walk a guy to get to a pitcher

98
00:05:48,333 --> 00:05:50,814
[SPEAKER_03]: And of course, on the other hand, there's a lot of good hitting pitchers.

99
00:05:52,916 --> 00:05:54,617
[SPEAKER_03]: And I'm sure that some of them...

100
00:05:55,417 --> 00:05:57,058
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm sure that some of them resent that.

101
00:05:58,559 --> 00:06:00,180
[SPEAKER_03]: We had a couple of pretty good hitting pitchers.

102
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:01,621
[SPEAKER_03]: Ted Lyons was a good hitting pitcher.

103
00:06:02,261 --> 00:06:03,382
[SPEAKER_03]: Thurston was a good hitter.

104
00:06:05,223 --> 00:06:05,684
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

105
00:06:08,125 --> 00:06:10,667
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think the National League will ever adopt it.

106
00:06:10,687 --> 00:06:12,348
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think.

107
00:06:12,808 --> 00:06:15,070
[SPEAKER_02]: My objection is it distorts the records.

108
00:06:15,931 --> 00:06:18,271
[SPEAKER_02]: You're so much interested in records, you know.

109
00:06:18,291 --> 00:06:20,072
[SPEAKER_03]: To me, it does something to the game.

110
00:06:20,152 --> 00:06:21,752
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it changes the game, too.

111
00:06:23,532 --> 00:06:24,653
[SPEAKER_03]: I've noticed one thing.

112
00:06:27,153 --> 00:06:29,833
[SPEAKER_03]: That these, of course, it's more of a business now.

113
00:06:31,874 --> 00:06:39,635
[SPEAKER_03]: Most of all these players have got lawyers negotiating salaries for them and all that sort of stuff.

114
00:06:39,655 --> 00:06:44,476
[SPEAKER_03]: That part of it's changed considerably, where we used to do our own negotiations.

115
00:06:45,122 --> 00:06:48,064
[SPEAKER_02]: You didn't send your agent to talk to Grabner, did you?

116
00:06:49,465 --> 00:06:50,405
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

117
00:06:51,686 --> 00:07:02,073
[SPEAKER_03]: And then on the other hand, to me, it just seems to me like a player is looking for help all the time.

118
00:07:03,874 --> 00:07:05,215
[SPEAKER_03]: The pitcher's out there pitching.

119
00:07:05,235 --> 00:07:07,977
[SPEAKER_03]: If he gets some little jammies, he's looking for help.

120
00:07:08,937 --> 00:07:10,959
[SPEAKER_03]: There's always pitchers come in.

121
00:07:11,259 --> 00:07:13,100
[SPEAKER_03]: When I played, while you went out there,

122
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:17,607
[SPEAKER_03]: you better make up your mind, you're going to pitch that ball again.

123
00:07:21,250 --> 00:07:25,653
[SPEAKER_03]: And if a guy got hurt, he had to get hurt awful bad to not play.

124
00:07:26,133 --> 00:07:28,015
[SPEAKER_03]: Now if a guy gets a little bump, he's out.

125
00:07:29,656 --> 00:07:30,477
[SPEAKER_03]: I've noticed that.

126
00:07:39,624 --> 00:07:40,184
[SPEAKER_03]: Personally,

127
00:07:42,510 --> 00:07:50,555
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think, I like to see a guy get all they can get, but honest to God, to me, they ain't a ballplayer living worth the money they're getting.

128
00:07:51,775 --> 00:07:52,095
[SPEAKER_03]: Really.

129
00:07:55,497 --> 00:08:07,744
[SPEAKER_02]: I would think players who played in your time would feel kind of disturbed about the money and the attitudes of these modern players.

130
00:08:07,764 --> 00:08:09,345
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it don't bother me any.

131
00:08:09,605 --> 00:08:10,826
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, I can understand

132
00:08:13,274 --> 00:08:14,855
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I played right side of Shaw.

133
00:08:14,895 --> 00:08:15,835
[SPEAKER_03]: I did all the catching.

134
00:08:15,875 --> 00:08:17,696
[SPEAKER_03]: He was making $35,000 a year.

135
00:08:20,938 --> 00:08:21,799
[SPEAKER_03]: Right there at the end.

136
00:08:22,439 --> 00:08:23,019
[SPEAKER_03]: As a player.

137
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:25,180
[SPEAKER_03]: And I was making five.

138
00:08:26,921 --> 00:08:30,143
[SPEAKER_03]: He was one of the very few that ever made that much money in Chicago.

139
00:08:30,804 --> 00:08:31,644
[SPEAKER_03]: Collins might have.

140
00:08:32,284 --> 00:08:34,285
[SPEAKER_03]: With Comiskey paying the bill.

141
00:08:37,512 --> 00:08:39,957
[SPEAKER_03]: In fact, Shaw came to me when he was managing.

142
00:08:39,977 --> 00:08:42,983
[SPEAKER_03]: Wanted to know if I was making enough money.

143
00:08:44,045 --> 00:08:47,050
[SPEAKER_03]: He offered to go at the front for me, and I told him I was happy.

144
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:51,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Forget it.

145
00:08:53,562 --> 00:09:00,445
[SPEAKER_03]: But, down to my heart, there ain't nobody worth the money they claim they're making, if they're making it.

146
00:09:01,206 --> 00:09:04,067
[SPEAKER_03]: And I've got no reason to think that they don't make it.

147
00:09:04,187 --> 00:09:06,608
[SPEAKER_02]: Troubles me is attitudes of players, too.

148
00:09:06,648 --> 00:09:10,049
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't seem to care too much about the game.

149
00:09:10,069 --> 00:09:11,710
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, what about attitudes in your team?

150
00:09:11,910 --> 00:09:13,391
[SPEAKER_03]: You know what brought all that in?

151
00:09:13,651 --> 00:09:14,471
[SPEAKER_02]: The colored people.

152
00:09:19,513 --> 00:09:22,215
[SPEAKER_03]: You just, look, they resented.

153
00:09:24,656 --> 00:09:31,138
[SPEAKER_03]: Like this Jackson with the Oakland A's and all them guys in a fight every day.

154
00:09:32,519 --> 00:09:34,240
[SPEAKER_03]: Fighting among themselves all the time.

155
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:36,221
[SPEAKER_03]: Dissension all over.

156
00:09:36,281 --> 00:09:39,542
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know how in the name of God they could win.

157
00:09:39,642 --> 00:09:45,505
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you hear a player, he just, he doesn't get what he wants.

158
00:09:47,034 --> 00:09:55,416
[SPEAKER_02]: quit and go into his business, you know, and it seemed to me in the old days the players just liked to play the game, or didn't care about the money.

159
00:09:55,436 --> 00:09:57,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Just get out in that field was a thrill.

160
00:09:57,757 --> 00:10:00,238
[SPEAKER_02]: I asked Lefty Grove, what was your greatest thrill in baseball?

161
00:10:00,698 --> 00:10:02,378
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, going out to the ballpark every day.

162
00:10:04,779 --> 00:10:07,060
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll never forget that son of a gun, that Grove.

163
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,720
[SPEAKER_03]: When he first came up, he was wild.

164
00:10:11,341 --> 00:10:14,222
[SPEAKER_03]: And brother, he had everybody standing on one foot back here.

165
00:10:17,270 --> 00:10:18,951
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll never forget the first time I hit at him.

166
00:10:19,312 --> 00:10:19,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah?

167
00:10:19,852 --> 00:10:20,572
[SPEAKER_03]: What time did you do?

168
00:10:20,592 --> 00:10:21,873
[SPEAKER_03]: Holy Jesus.

169
00:10:24,955 --> 00:10:28,197
[SPEAKER_03]: He threw one behind me and one down on my feet.

170
00:10:29,778 --> 00:10:30,539
[SPEAKER_03]: He finally got...

171
00:11:04,672 --> 00:11:06,293
[SPEAKER_03]: I can tell you a pretty good story about Walter.

172
00:11:06,313 --> 00:11:16,577
[SPEAKER_03]: When I first went up, all the way up, I was there, we was in Chicago about, just about two weeks, and we went on an eastern trip.

173
00:11:17,997 --> 00:11:27,541
[SPEAKER_03]: And all on that trip, all I heard was, Walter Johnson, and you're gonna have to catch again, Walter Johnson, because you hit left-handed, and Shaw kicked right-handed.

174
00:11:28,621 --> 00:11:31,182
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it had me scared to death before I ever got there.

175
00:11:32,557 --> 00:11:35,498
[SPEAKER_03]: So sure enough, Walter Johnson is pitching this day.

176
00:11:37,058 --> 00:11:38,698
[SPEAKER_03]: And they waited until the very last minute.

177
00:11:38,718 --> 00:11:41,219
[SPEAKER_03]: They said, you're catching.

178
00:11:42,259 --> 00:11:42,859
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, okay.

179
00:11:43,759 --> 00:11:47,480
[SPEAKER_03]: So the first time at bat, nobody on.

180
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:54,041
[SPEAKER_03]: And I hit the doggonest line drive you ever saw with a shortstop head for two bases.

181
00:11:55,521 --> 00:11:57,141
[SPEAKER_03]: And I was a cocky little devil.

182
00:11:58,562 --> 00:12:00,162
[SPEAKER_03]: And I come in on the bench and I said,

183
00:12:01,376 --> 00:12:04,197
[SPEAKER_03]: If that's all he's got, he ain't gonna worry me, ain't he?

184
00:12:06,957 --> 00:12:08,738
[SPEAKER_03]: And I sure made a mistake when I said that.

185
00:12:10,118 --> 00:12:17,080
[SPEAKER_03]: The next time I went up to bat, there was a couple of guys on base, and I never saw the first three balls he threw, and I was out.

186
00:12:20,281 --> 00:12:22,522
[SPEAKER_03]: I went in the bench, I said, I changed my mind.

187
00:12:23,502 --> 00:12:24,002
[SPEAKER_02]: He had it.

188
00:12:25,830 --> 00:12:27,491
[SPEAKER_02]: But he was a real gentleman.

189
00:12:27,651 --> 00:12:30,892
[SPEAKER_02]: You think he might have let you hit that first pitch, that first time out?

190
00:12:33,133 --> 00:12:36,234
[SPEAKER_03]: He was just throwing them in there, trying to get me out.

191
00:12:36,674 --> 00:12:37,774
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, he was just throwing them in there.

192
00:12:37,794 --> 00:12:43,056
[SPEAKER_02]: I heard this, that when there were no people on base, he didn't do that much.

193
00:12:43,256 --> 00:12:44,937
[SPEAKER_02]: But when he had to, he did.

194
00:12:44,977 --> 00:12:46,017
[SPEAKER_03]: He did, and he had it.

195
00:12:46,697 --> 00:12:49,878
[SPEAKER_03]: And you didn't have to be afraid of him, because he wasn't wild.

196
00:12:49,998 --> 00:12:51,339
[SPEAKER_03]: He was right over that plate.

197
00:12:52,519 --> 00:12:54,280
[SPEAKER_03]: But he really had something on that ball.

198
00:12:55,062 --> 00:13:09,206
[SPEAKER_02]: I've heard it said of him that he could have won a lot more games, or he'd won a lot as it was, but he could have been even more effective if he once in a while did brush a batter back.

199
00:13:10,446 --> 00:13:14,807
[SPEAKER_02]: But he would never do that because he was afraid he might hit him and he knew how fast he threw.

200
00:13:15,627 --> 00:13:17,508
